// help.grif - GRIF format
@abs(x)
	Returns the absolute value of "x". Returns 0 if "x" is "". Returns an error if "x" is not numeric or null.
@add(x,y)
	Returns the value of integer "x" plus integer "y". If "x" or "y" is "", uses 0. Returns an error if either are not numeric or null.
@addlist(key,x)
	Adds value "x" to the end of list named "key".
@addto(key,x)
	Adds the integer value of "x" to the value in "key" and saves back to "key". If "x" is "" or value in "key" is "" or null, uses 0. Returns an error if either are not numeric or "x" is null.
@and
	In @if or @while, this separates multiple conditions. If the previous condition is false, it short-circuits and returns false. It does not check the rest of the conditions but jumps to the next @elseif, @else, or @endif. Otherwise it continues on and checks the next condition.
@bitwiseand(x,y)
	Returns a bitwise AND of integer values "x" and "y". For example, @bitwiseand(7,2) = 2, since 7 = 111 binary and 2 = 010 binary and the result is 010 binary. Returns an error if either are not numeric or are less than zero.
@bitwiseor(x,y)
	Returns a bitwise OR of integer values "x" and "y". For example, @bitwiseor(7,2) = 7, since 7 = 111 binary and 2 = 010 binary and the result is 111 binary. Returns an error if either are not numeric or are less than zero.
@bitwisexor(x,y)
	Returns a bitwise XOR of integer values "x" and "y". For example, @bitwiseand(7,2) = 5, since 7 = 111 binary and 2 = 010 binary and the result is 101 binary. Returns an error if either are not numeric or are less than zero.
@cleararray(key)
	Clears all values from the array named "key".
@clearbit(x,b)
	Sets bit "b" in integer value "x" to 0, with b = 0 to 30. Lowest bit is on the right. Returns an error if either are not numeric, below zero, or "b" is over 30.
@clearlist(key)
	Clears all values from list named "key".
@comment(value)
	Used for comments in scripts. This is completely ignored. Surround 'value' with quotes.\s
@concat(x,y,...)
	Concatenates the values supplied and returns the result.
@contains(x,y)
	Returns true/false if string "x" contains string "y". Case is ignored.
@datetime(format)
	Returns the current local datetime using a valid .NET format pattern. Use quotes around the pattern. Timezone in returned in ExtraValue. For example, @datetime("MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm") might return "01/17/2020 15:23", and the ExtraValue might be "-05:00" for US Eastern time zone.
@datetime(format,utc)
	Returns the current universal datetime using a valid .NET format pattern. Use quotes around the pattern. A timezone of "UTC" in returned in ExtraValue. For example, @datetime("MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm") might return "01/17/2020 15:23". The ExtraValue will always be "UTC".
@debug(value)
	This command is enabled if the value in "system.debug" = "true". Otherwise it is ignored. It outputs the value plus a newline. Surround the value with quotes if it is a literal.
@div(x,y)
	Returns integer result of integer "x" divided by integer "y". Returns an error if either are not numeric or "y" equals zero.
@divto(key,x)
	Sets key to integer result of value in "key" divided by "x". Returns an error if either are not numeric or "x" equals zero.
@do
	In a @while loop, marks the end of conditions and starts the statement block.
@else
	If all conditions in @if and all @elseif are false, the statements in the @else block are run. @else is optional.
@elseif
	If all conditions in an @if are false, starts a new set of conditions. There can be multiple @elseif condition blocks. @elseif is optional.
@endfor
	End of a @for loop.
@endforeachkey
	End of a @foreachkey loop.
@endforeachlist
	End of a @foreachlist loop.
@endif
	End of an @if block.
@endwhile
	End of a @while loop.
@eq(x,y)
	Returns true/false if "x" equals "y". Compares as numeric if possible. Otherwise it compares as strings ignoring case.
@exec(value)
	Executes the value as a script. Must surround the value with quotes to prevent it from being evaluated incorrectly.
@flipbit(x,b)
	Sets bit "b" in "x" to the opposite 0/1, with b = 0 to 30. Lowest bit is on the right. Returns an error if either are not numeric, below zero, or "b" is over 30.
@for(x,start,end)
	Runs the @for loop, replacing "$x" inside with values from start to end inclusive as increasing integers.
@foreachkey(x,prefix,[suffix])
	Gets a list of keys with the specified prefix and optional suffix. Executes the @foreachkey loop, replacing "$x" with the part of the key after removing prefix and optional suffix.
@foreachlist(x,listname)
	Executes the @foreachlist loop. For each item in listname, replace "$x" with the value from the list.
@format(pattern,x0,x1,...)
	Replace the pattern values "{0}", "{1}", ... with the value x0, x1, ...
@frombinary(x)
	Converts the binary string x to an integer. @frombinary(11111111) = 255.
@fromhex(x)
	Converts the hex string x to an integer. @fromhex(FF) = 255.
@ge(x,y)
	Returns "true" if "x" is greater than or equal to "y". If both are numeric, they are compared as numbers, otherwise they are compared as strings. If "y" is null, the answer is "true" since null is less than all other values. If "x" is null and "y" is not null, the answer is "false".
@get(key)
	Gets the value for "key". If the value is a script, it is not executed and returned as-is.
@getarray(key,y,x)
	Gets the value in array "key" at position row "y", column "x". Arrays use "row,column" notation. Returns "null" if "y,x" is beyond the end of the array. Throws an error if "y" or "x" is negative.
@getbit(x,b)
	Returns the bit "b" in "x", with b = 0 to 30. Lowest bit is on the right. Throws an error if either are not numeric, below zero, or "b" is over 30.
@getchar(
	todo
@getinchannel
	Get the value sent from calling program sent in "#INCHANNEL;".
@getlist(key,x)
	Gets the value in list "key" at position "x". Returns "null" if "x" is beyond the end of the list. Throws an error if "x" is negative.
@getvalue(key)
	Gets the value for "key". If the value is a script, it is executed and the final value is returned.
@golabel(x)
	Jumps to @label(x).\n\nUse with caution: Jumping within a block of statements or out of an @if/@for.../@while block works, but jumping into another block of statements or into a deeper level may have unexpected results.
@gt(x,y)
	Returns "true" if "x" is greater than "y". If both are numeric, they are compared as numbers, otherwise they are compared as strings. If "x" is not null and "y" is null, the answer is "true" since null is less than all other values. If "x" is null the answer is "false".
@if
	Begins an "@if" block. The full format of an "@if" block is: "@if <conditions> @then <statements> [@elseif <conditions> @then <statements> ...] [@else <statements>] @endif". There can be any number of @elseif sections. "<conditions>" consist of one or more values or functions which return truthy or falsey values. Multiple conditions are connected with "@and" or "@or", and may start with "@not" to reverse the value. "<statements>" are a block of one or more "@" functions. "@if" may be nested within other "@if" <statement> blocks.
@inlist(key,value)
	Returns true/false if list "key" contains an item "value". Case is ignored. Same as "@listcontains".
@insertatlist(
	todo
@isbool(x)
	Returns "true" if "x" is truthy or falsey, or "false" otherwise. Truthy values are "true", "t", "yes", "y", "1", "-1", ignoring case. Falsey values are "false", "f", "no", "n", "0", and "", ignoring case. "null" is neither truthy or falsey and will return "false".
@isfalse(x)
	Returns "true" if "x" is falsey or "false" otherwise. Falsey values are "false", "null", "0", and "", ignoring case. Any invalid value will return an error.
@isnull(x)
	Returns "true" if "x" is "null" or an empty string, "false" otherwise.
@isnumber(x)
	Returns "true" if the string value "x" can be converted into a 64-bit signed integer value. Returns "false" otherwise. Known as "long" or "bigint" in some languages.
@isscript(key)
	Returns "true" if the value for "key" is a script, i.e. starts with "@". Returns "false" otherwise.
@istrue(x)
	Returns "true" if "x" is truthy or "false" otherwise. Truthy values are "true", "1", and "-1", ignoring case. "null" will return "false". Any invalid value will return an error.
@label(x)
	A label named "x" within a script. This is the target of "@golabel(x)" or an "@ongolabel(value,..,x,...)" command. Execution will continue with the command after the label. "x" must be a constant value, not a script. If there is more than one label with the same name, the first one is always used.
@le(x,y)
	Returns "true" if "x" is less than or equal to "y". If both are numeric, they are compared as numbers, otherwise they are compared as strings. If "x" is null, the answer is "true". If "x" is not null and "y" is null, the answer is "false".
@len(
	todo
@listcontains(key,value)
	Returns true/false if list "key" contains an item "value". Case is ignored. Same as "@inlist".
@listcontainsall(key1,key2)
	Returns true/false if list "key1" contains all values in list "key2". Case is ignored.
@listlength(key)
	Returns the number of items in list "key".
@lower(x)
	Converts all the alphabetic characters in string "x" to lowercase.
@lt(x,y)
	Returns "true" if "x" is less than "y". If both are numeric, they are compared as numbers, otherwise they are compared as strings. If "x" is null and "y" is not null, the answer is "true". If "y" is null, the answer is "false".
@max(x1,x2,...)
	Returns the maximum numeric value for all parameters. Throws an error if any are not numeric.
@min(x1,x2,...)
	Returns the minimum numeric value for all parameters. Throws an error if any are not numeric.
@mod(x,y)
	Returns the numeric value of "x" modulus "y". This is the remainder if "x" were to be divided by "y". If "x" is negative, the answer is negative.
@modto(key,x)
	Takes the numeric value from "key", calculates the modulus using "x", and stores the answer back to "key". This is the remainder if the value in "key" were to be divided by "x".  If the value in "key" is negative, the answer is negative.
@msg(key)
	Displays the value at "key", followed by a newline.
@mul(x,y)
	Returns the value of integer "x" times integer "y". If "x" or "y" is "", uses 0. Returns an error if either are not numeric or null.
@multo(key,x)
	Takes the numeric value from "key", multiplies it by "x", and stores the answer back into "key".
@ne(x,y)
	Returns "true" if x is not equal to y, or "false" otherwise. If x and y are numbers, compares as numbers. If not it compares as strings ignoring case.
@neg(x)
	Returns the negative of the numeric value x.
@negto(key)
	Takes the numeric value at "key", negates it, and stores it back to "key".
@nl
	Add a newline character to the result.
@not
	In "@if" or "@while", negates the following condition.
@null(x)
	Returns "true" if "x" is null or an empty string, "false" otherwise. Identical to "@isnull(x)".
@ongolabel(value,label1,label2...)
	Goes to a specific label based on the value. If value=1, control is passed to @label(label1), value=2 to @label(label2), and so on. If the value is less than 1 or more than the number of labels, or if the label is not found, control just drops through to the next command.
@or
	In "@if" or "@while", this separates multiple conditions. If the previous condition is true, it short-circuits and returns true. It does not check the rest of the conditions but jumps to @then/@do. Otherwise it continues on and checks the next condition.
@rand(value)
	Returns true or false if a random integer value from 0 to 99 is less than "value". Used for percentage tests, so "@rand(50)" will be true about 50% of the time. "@rand(0)" is always false and "@rand(100)" is always true.
@removeatlist(
	todo
@replace(
	todo
@return
	Exit the current script immediately, returning any results.
@rnd(value)
	Returns a random integer value from 0 to "value" minus one. "@rnd(0)" is always zero.
@script(key)
	Runs the script stored at "key". If the value is not a script, it is output as text.
@set(key,value)
	Sets the value for "key" to "value". This happens on the top GROD layer. See "@setlayer()".
@setarray(key,y,x,value)
	Sets the value in array "key" at position row "y", column "x". Arrays use "row,column" notation. Fills any preceeding values with "null" if "y,x" is beyond the end of the array. Throws an error if "y" or "x" is negative.
@setbit(x,b)
	Sets the bit "b" in "x" to 1, with b = 0 to 30. Lowest bit is on the right. Returns an error if either are not numeric, below zero, or "b" is over 30.
@setchar(
	todo
@setlayer(layer,key,value)
	For a named GROD layer, sets "key" to "value". If the named layer does not exists, one is added under the current top layer. This is the way to change values on layers other than the top GROD layer. See "@set()".
@setlist(
	todo
@setoutchannel(
	todo
@sub(x,y)
	Returns the value of integer "x" minus integer "y". If "x" or "y" is "", uses 0. Returns an error if either are not numeric or null.
@substring(
	todo
@subto(
	todo
@swap(key1,key2)
	Swaps the values between key1 and key2.
@then
	Ends the condition checking for @if and @elseif sections. If the conditions were true, executes the next block. Otherwise skips to the next @elseif or @else block, or skips past the @endif.
@tobinary(x)
	Converts the integer x to a binary string. @tobinary(255) = 11111111.
@tohex(x)
	Converts the integer x to a hex string. @tohex(255) = FF.
@trim(x)
	Trims any whitespace from the beginning and ending of string x.
@upper(x)
	Converts all the alphabetic characters in string x to uppercase.
@while
	todo
@write(value1,value2,...)
	Writes out the concatination of value1+value2+... Note that this is returned as a list of separate values, not a single string.
@writeline(value1,value2,...)
	Writes out the concatination of value1+value2+..., with a newline at the end. Note that this is returned as a list of separate values, not a single string.
Lists and Arrays
	A list is stored as a set of values separated by commas, such as "1,2,3". The key is the name of the list. The first item is at position 0, so "@getlist(name,0)" would return "1" in this example.\n\nArrays are stored as a set of lists, one for each row in the array. The "y" parameter indicates the row, and "x" the position in that row's list. The keys for each array row have the foramt "name.row.y", with "name" as the name of the array and "y" as the row number. Arrays are sparse, with any empty rows not stored and items beyond the end of a row returning "".
Scripts
	A script is a value which starts with "@" as the start of some function key. There can be one or many statements, such as @if blocks, @for blocks, etc. The statements will process until it reaches the end of the script, or it reaches a "@return" statement, or it throws an error.\n\nThe complete set of all results from the script are returned together, not individually as they occur.\n\nScripts do not need to be formatted, but they will be when stored in a GRIF file for human readability. In a JSON file, they are stored as an unformatted string.\n\nWithin a script, using a key which starts with underline ("_") signifies a local variable only available in that copy of the script. If another copy of the same script is started, it won't have the same values.\n\nScripts are parsed from the string value into a collection of tokens which is held in memory while processing, then discarded. You can alter a running script stored in the dictionary, but it won't take effect until the next time it is run.
User-defined functions
	You can define your own functions/routines by starting the key with "@". If there are parameters, you would describe them in the key, like "@func(x,y)". Within the script the values "$x" and "$y" would be replaced by the values sent in "x" and "y".\n\nYou can define a function without parameters, such as "@func2". These could do any normal commands starting with "@", or they could be a plain text value. For instance, "@darkmsg" = "You are in the dark.", could be used as "@write(@darkmsg)".\n\nA user-defined function can be used anywhere a built-in function is allowed.\n\nThe keys for user-defined functions cannot have spaces or quotes. They cannot have an "@" after the begining one. They cannot duplicate the names of built-in functions, even with different numbers of parameters. They need one start and one end parenthesis if there are parameters. The parameters should start with a letter and use only letters and numbers, and are separated by commas.
Game Dictionaries
	A GRIF game is stored in one or more dictionaries (saved as *.GRIF or *.JSON files). The dictionaries are key/value pairs, with both the key and value being strings.\n\nYou can put an entire game into a single file, with all values and scripts to make it work. You can  split a game across multiple files and load them all in a stack. A "*.GRIFSTACK" file contains a list of filenames of the files to load in a stack. Or you can list them on the command line for GRIF (either with or without "-m"/"--mod" arguments).\n\nHaving separate files will allow you to store different kinds of values in each, such as global routines, special routines, room data, item data, and values. Whatever makes sense to you.\n\nYou can create "mod" files which modify the game (hence the "-m"/"--mod" argument) that change how it works without changing the basic game files. Maybe put all written string values into one file, and make mod files for other languages using the same keys, so your messages display in French, for instance.
Game Overlay
	Once the game is loaded in memory, a special dictionary is added to the top of the stack, known as the "overlay". This contains all added or changed key/value pairs since the game started. Using the "@set(...)" command writes only to the overlay, while a "@get(...)" will start at the overlay looking for a key, falling down through the stack until it is found. This makes the overlay exactly all the data needed for saving the game state.